    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(12))  # C0..C11\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(23, 89), (57, 87), (37, 33), (9, 4), (60, 84)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(50, 53), (52, 38), (63, 57), (40, 11), (56, 82), (34, 38), (46, 23), (69, 96), (30, 71), (96, 88), (49, 93), (83, 48)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [72.0, 136.0, 163.0, 97.0, 174.0]\n    customer_demands = [50.0, 35.0, 12.0, 15.0, 32.0, 22.0, 42.0, 23.0, 42.0, 12.0, 25.0, 30.0]\n    fixed_costs = [113.0, 483.0, 386.0, 341.0, 460.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point with our distribution network. We have five candidate warehouse locations and twelve customer locations, and we need to choose which sites to open and how to ship product so that every customer is served in the most cost-effective way.\n\nThe candidate warehouse sites are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (23, 89)\n  - F1: (57, 87)\n  - F2: (37, 33)\n  - F3: (9, 4)\n  - F4: (60, 84), and the customer delivery points are given in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (50, 53)\n  - C1: (52, 38)\n  - C2: (63, 57)\n  - C3: (40, 11)\n  - C4: (56, 82)\n  - C5: (34, 38)\n  - C6: (46, 23)\n  - C7: (69, 96)\n  - C8: (30, 71)\n  - C9: (96, 88)\n  - C10: (49, 93)\n  - C11: (83, 48). To be explicit about how we measure distance: distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nOpening any warehouse comes with an upfront fixed cost as listed in [113.0, 483.0, 386.0, 341.0, 460.0]. Moving product from a warehouse to a customer is charged by distance and volume at 0.5 per kilometer per unit shipped. A facility can only ship if it is opened.\n\nOperationally, each candidate site has a finite handling capacity, summarized in [72.0, 136.0, 163.0, 97.0, 174.0]. On the demand side, each customer has a required volume that must be fully satisfied, shown in [50.0, 35.0, 12.0, 15.0, 32.0, 22.0, 42.0, 23.0, 42.0, 12.0, 25.0, 30.0]. Our shipment plan must respect these capacity limits while meeting all customer demand.\n\nThe business goal is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing what we spend to open warehouses with what we spend to ship product. Opening more sites can shorten delivery distances and improve service coverage but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but may lengthen hauls and put more pressure on capacity. Using the data above, we will recommend which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments so we achieve the lowest combined opening and shipping cost while meeting every customer\u2019s needs.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point with our distribution network. We have five candidate warehouse locations and twelve customer locations, and we need to choose which sites to open and how to ship product so that every customer is served in the most cost-effective way.\n\nThe candidate warehouse sites are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer delivery points are given in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. To be explicit about how we measure distance: distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates.\n\nOpening any warehouse comes with an upfront fixed cost as listed in {FIXED_COSTS}. Moving product from a warehouse to a customer is charged by distance and volume at {TRANSPORT_RATE} per kilometer per unit shipped. A facility can only ship if it is opened.\n\nOperationally, each candidate site has a finite handling capacity, summarized in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}. On the demand side, each customer has a required volume that must be fully satisfied, shown in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}. Our shipment plan must respect these capacity limits while meeting all customer demand.\n\nThe business goal is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing what we spend to open warehouses with what we spend to ship product. Opening more sites can shorten delivery distances and improve service coverage but increases fixed costs; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but may lengthen hauls and put more pressure on capacity. Using the data above, we will recommend which warehouses to open and how to allocate shipments so we achieve the lowest combined opening and shipping cost while meeting every customer\u2019s needs."
